  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Envisioning App Store Functionality**\n\nI\'m currently focused on defining the core functionalities of this app/game store website. The aim is a user experience mirroring Google Play, but with a modern, elegant design. Browsing, detailed app views, and downloads are essential. I\'m also planning the admin panel features.\n\n\n**Defining Backend and Security**\n\nI\'ve outlined the critical backend infrastructure now. Supabase will handle the database, user auth, and role management. Cloudinary will handle image uploads securely via a backend proxy, keeping credentials hidden. For the admin side, I\'m setting up a dashboard with game management capabilities: adding, editing, deleting entries with all the necessary fields. Security is paramount, requiring password hashing, API protection, and admin panel access control.\n\n\n**Refining Infrastructure Details**\n\nI\'m now detailing the backend setup further. I\'ve ruled out Firebase. I\'m focusing on Supabase for the database, user auth, and admin login. Cloudinary will still handle image uploads. I\'m considering an Express backend in `server.ts` to proxy Supabase API calls. This allows graceful fallback to local data. Mock data will be implemented when no database keys are given. The idea of adding Oauth, focus-mode, gemini-api, workspace-integration, or shadcn-ui is dropped for now. Image generation is only for mock images.\n\n\n**Implementing Local Fallback**\n\nI\'m now implementing the local fallback mechanism for Supabase and Cloudinary. The key is to ensure the app runs immediately with a mock database in local storage, simulating Supabase Auth and data storage. Real integrations with both services will be written, and only when the keys are configured, will the actual calls execute. UI warnings will gracefully guide the user in setting up the credentials. This way, a fully-functional experience is guaranteed with a seamless transition to the real thing.\n\n\n**Establishing Server-Side Proxy**\n\nI\'m now fully committed to a full-stack architecture, and I\'m focused on setting up the server-side proxy. After reviewing the guidelines, I see a strong argument to use lazy initialization and handle missing keys gracefully, which the full-stack enables. I\'m going to create the `server.ts` file now. This is where I\'ll implement Express to proxy requests and hide any sensitive API keys.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 377354242
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
