    thinking: "**Analyzing Supabase Setup**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics. I\'m focusing on how `isSupabaseConfigured` determines if the Supabase URL and Anon Key are valid. Once confirmed, a client is created using `createClient`. The core now, is to understand what exact values it uses. I need to be sure that I am aware of these.\n\n\n**Investigating URL Errors**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a specific error: \"Invalid path specified in request URL.\" It seems this can occur in `@supabase/supabase-js`, specifically when the URL has extra slashes, paths, or is malformed. I\'m considering scenarios where `supabaseUrl` might include an unexpected path, trailing spaces, or invalid characters. I\'m also examining how paths in requests to `supabase.from(\"games\")` could contribute. I need to ensure a clean URL structure.\n\n\n**Uncovering Supabase URL Issues**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial discovery. While `isSupabaseConfigured` trims the `url` and `key`, `createClient` uses the untrimmed `supabaseUrl` and `supabaseAnonKey`. This explains the \"Invalid path specified in request URL\" error \342\200\223 environment variables with whitespace, such as trailing spaces or newline characters, are not addressed. I\'m now certain this is a major cause of the issue, which wasn\'t previously clear.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Root Cause**\n\nI\'ve hit the jackpot! My hypothesis solidifies: the \"Invalid path specified in request URL\" stems from malformed URLs. Specifically, whitespace (trailing spaces, carriage returns like `%0D`, or newline characters like `%0A`) in environment variables like `.env` leads to invalid paths in requests to the Supabase API. This manifests when the `supabaseUrl` gets passed, unfiltered, to `createClient` and subsequent `new URL(url)` calls within `@supabase/supabase-js`, or with the fetch client. These corrupted paths cause failures through PostgREST/GoTrue\'s Kong gateway, confirming my suspicions.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will view the occurrences of `supabaseUrl` and `supabaseAnonKey` in `server.ts` to see how we can properly sanitize and trim them."
