    web_documents {
      url: "https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGO1bUs44LNqbd9QDe9WIR_MS6Cja1W0eGK45LKOWQijsfDH1_7OeuN-kJi6cS7E_wPNBS-H1HoEwl3LrpDk8IWHquOXF4aK24-hgesvCdfd0yzLfRdmatWQdk2RPFlhrct6xLmHIQ9l4P--ItU3OAfls_Bl8JtCl1BFg=="
      title: "postgrest.org"
    }
    summary: "The \"Invalid path specified in request URL\" error in PostgREST typically indicates that the requested URL does not correspond to an exposed and accessible database object (table, view, or function) or that the URL itself is malformed according to PostgREST\'s grammar.\n\nHere are the common causes and solutions for this error:\n\n*   **Incorrect Table, View, or Function Name**\n    *   **Cause:** The most frequent reason is that the path in your URL does not precisely match the name of an existing table, view, or RPC function in your exposed database schema. PostgREST exposes these database objects as top-level API endpoints.[1][2][3]\n    *   **Solution:** Double-check the spelling and case of the table, view, or function name in your URL against your database schema. For instance, if you have a table named `users`, the endpoint should be `/users`.\n\n*   **Schema Configuration Issues**\n    *   **Cause:** The database object you are trying to access might not reside in one of the schemas configured for PostgREST to expose (defined by the `db-schemas` setting). PostgREST will only expose objects within these specified schemas. Additionally, the database role used by PostgREST might lack the necessary `USAGE` privilege on the schema or `SELECT`/`INSERT`/etc. permissions on the specific table/view/function.[4][5] Critical schemas like `pg_catalog` and `information_schema` are not allowed to be exposed directly.[4]\n    *   **Solution:**\n        *   Verify that the `db-schemas` configuration in your PostgREST instance includes the schema where your target object is located.[5]\n        *   Ensure that the database role PostgREST uses has the appropriate `USAGE` privilege on the schema and permissions on the specific table, view, or function.\n        *   If using multiple exposed schemas, ensure you are specifying the correct schema, potentially via `Accept-Profile` or `Content-Profile` HTTP headers, or through a URL prefix if configured.[4][6][7]\n\n*   **URL Encoding Problems**\n    *   **Cause:** If your table names, column names, or filter values contain special characters (such as spaces, Unicode characters, or PostgREST reserved characters like `,`, `.`, `:`, `(`, `)`), they must be percent-encoded in the URL.[1] Some HTTP client libraries might automatically encode URLs, which can sometimes lead to incorrect encoding if not handled carefully.[1]\n    *   **Solution:** Ensure all special characters in your URL path or query parameters are correctly percent-encoded. For example, a table named `Order Items` would be accessed as `/Order%20Items`.[1]\n\n*   **Incorrect Function Parameters for RPC Calls**\n    *   **Cause:** When calling database functions (RPC), if the parameters provided in the URL or request body do not match the function\'s signature (e.g., wrong data type, missing parameters, or incorrect array formatting), PostgREST may not be able to find the matching function, leading to an \"Invalid path\" error.[8]\n    *   **Solution:** Confirm that the parameters you are sending, including their types and formatting (e.g., using `%7B` and `%7D` for `{` and `}` for text arrays), align with the expected arguments of your PostgreSQL function.[8]\n\n*   **Unsupported Nested Routes**\n    *   **Cause:** PostgREST offers one-level deep routes for tables and views. It does not natively support deeply nested routes like `/films/1/director`.[2][3]\n    *   **Solution:** Instead of nested routes, PostgREST uses \"resource embedding\" to retrieve related information within a single request.[2][3] You would typically query the main resource (`/films`) and specify related resources to embed using the `select` query parameter.\n\n*   **Stale Schema Cache**\n    *   **Cause:** PostgREST maintains a cache of your database schema. If you\'ve made recent changes to your database (e.g., added a new table or function) and the PostgREST server hasn\'t reloaded its schema cache, it won\'t be aware of the new objects.[9]\n    *   **Solution:** Reload the PostgREST schema cache. This often involves sending a `SIGHUP` signal to the PostgREST process or restarting the PostgREST server.\n\n*   **OpenAPI Mode Configuration**\n    *   **Cause:** If the `openapi-mode` configuration is set to \"disabled\" and you attempt to access the API\'s root path, PostgREST might return a 404 error, which could be interpreted as an \"Invalid path.\"[9][10]\n    *   **Solution:** Check your `openapi-mode` configuration if you are trying to access the root path (`/`). If you intend to use the OpenAPI documentation, ensure it\'s not disabled.\n\nBy systematically checking these points, you should be able to identify and resolve the \"Invalid path specified in request URL\" error in your PostgREST application.\n\nSources:\n[1] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQECyBmXt2gtGb66MPZIRlIjxkw_7Hu_Ig1l64uUyWt5E2l0tQUPY54EVJAp8iYamWvPqSk-xHeAbW47AvUdkCn930lwRxVFP-TFc0D55PUc3426i4wqu2LeXI-wTLLhvsa5R1pev-qb1ri7b9mlMS65KVIaXcnRwzIM3L9J)\n[2] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFcIdaKku3S98mHla5Ha1-YMaY-ua3MPKC3ceB68bmh8YbF9ICemv_NdUKerBCZ3qUoHPN5-RiSmCeN6H3u0RAMrD2Y2hlDmbnLpBho9VzNjl409hY9mhlcQaS9RmzJAdl8oHIuXzPo3YecjmN9danCstt8uocl_EU=)\n[3] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGlp7pqcMaiVILujkl5VjZZXzDXPQHRahDDEWD0fuM1aFbUwGyJLYF3Wlzv59CldJtbiIO-x-wIv-foLdzuRi8uni_stdKGVWt1f25m5kVyoVlGIZUIs_h3-pAeLmNCbQFPykiYLQ==)\n[4] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFKJaWnMNJ7Acqtlbc6fCLHH7ee9GKAEroABrSse23Xh78cFuYE9eo0dy0zfW--a6sjhbvewh7yvfdOgQ328hFgKclcFYfhcvNAS5rnAYPPhVwIgF7yhLfiGT4GCeITrtKo1Co7AiPgpgGFKOkcqYd9yzesVYLBdUeGBNQ=)\n[5] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFwJ7rorxx4_OY_wToiA3AJNpl-YA9262uoevdGRJj4O9dIWDbDdMFK2i4o8I5yQhdK6O2XRFrZCydQtie1eEr4HJHvjNImT1SLvIPD9As-i8f2I7giVELBicYo_udAMmOmp2z7I-Q4CzmBDzDJFI5GFQSGTNppegQZnt4eEw==)\n[6] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHpdUAVaPRWxL0XynQs7VgRZ0QwxGKcgCB8L2m3OOJYUkCTU2Nc1w4r1HCFaKy-Q7RarxXUzS4VoY1gyiHARXl6K_tcw_k8DcG3MbBnKDDfr9D422CZCPKZujQRg0mKdVHCDy0giA1mhNT-1ViK)\n[7] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH2RPVx2yM9kvddNE-WxKMqVZ8kdIJuzA2lCaMcTUrgTLmTTkshNwkPFPCvcB1xQN6azoQhLVYwXRSkPhczeT6Cp_ML0EuhdECISfl0li985FSmQps3bKWct6isEsJklWoWmq8CX9Cdr1C3uJIf)\n[8] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHV4sTlwSPjruySgAPpvN4vszzJSqUzWDODeykrJ-u7O1IQTgyVoqsoX--tcxnJvNtlms5aFOm4CpctZgtYvV2QV4AQ9GUazr53TDCZkpZEdtHBtogd0vg2SJlz55VNteOS4T7Niy3M2cmUDZFdXnRBZ-UbwkTu4YxpeBOl8giEWT3KNeyRNckkUzLbipJNa2dPuCnm9cmT6TyS5VHt-VC_v2Eh)\n[9] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGJeiG4ZN4rZRzEskhc2KLirsmrPm3ie7qzd59AjU5K-GAZly0AmAu7QsJsQwhqzTWT11rNjpl-PkBSALNKMkYt3C6A7h3wzXDJBt8pEHrn7_xidjCxf4FwzdcA9kCdzAMEMm5_uSZG0ibVKDCLhIThaDAjV1Ix)\n[10] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGO1bUs44LNqbd9QDe9WIR_MS6Cja1W0eGK45LKOWQijsfDH1_7OeuN-kJi6cS7E_wPNBS-H1HoEwl3LrpDk8IWHquOXF4aK24-hgesvCdfd0yzLfRdmatWQdk2RPFlhrct6xLmHIQ9l4P--ItU3OAfls_Bl8JtCl1BFg==)"
