      title: "postgrest.org"
    }
    summary: "The error \"Invalid path specified in request URL\" in PostgREST typically indicates that the URL being used in the API request does not conform to PostgREST\'s expected structure or refers to a resource (table, view, or function) that doesn\'t exist or isn\'t accessible at that path[1][2].\n\nHere are common reasons and solutions:\n\n**1. Incorrect URL Structure or Resource Name:**\n*   **Typos:** Double-check the spelling of the table, view, or function name in the URL.\n*   **Case Sensitivity:** PostgreSQL (and thus PostgREST) can be case-sensitive with object names if they were created with quoted identifiers. Ensure the casing in your URL matches the database.\n*   **Missing Schema:** If your table/view/function is not in the default `public` schema, you might need to specify the schema in the PostgREST configuration or the URL, depending on your setup.\n*   **Non-existent Resource:** The table, view, or stored function you are trying to access might not exist in the database or in the schema PostgREST is configured to expose[2].\n*   **Reserved Characters:** If your filters include PostgREST reserved characters (`,`, `.`, `:`, `(`, `)`) you\'ll need to surround them in percent-encoded double quotes (`%22`) for correct processing[3].\n\n**2. Schema Cache Issues:**\n*   PostgREST relies on a schema cache to understand your database structure. If you\'ve recently made changes to your database schema (e.g., added a new table or function), PostgREST might be using an outdated cache. Reloading the schema cache can resolve this[4].\n\n**3. URL Encoding Problems:**\n*   Some HTTP client libraries might automatically encode URLs. If you are already percent-encoding characters like double quotes, the library might encode them again, leading to an incorrect path. In such cases, use double quotes directly instead of `%22`[3].\n\n**4. HTTP Method Restrictions:**\n*   When calling database functions via RPC (Remote Procedure Call), only `GET` and `POST` verbs are allowed. Using any other HTTP method for a function call will result in an \"Invalid path\" error[1][4].\n\n**5. Reverse Proxy Configuration:**\n*   If you are using a reverse proxy like Nginx in front of PostgREST, an incorrect proxy configuration can rewrite the URL path in a way that PostgREST doesn\'t understand, leading to this error[5]. Ensure your Nginx configuration correctly forwards the intended path to PostgREST.\n\n**6. API Root Path Access with Disabled OpenAPI:**\n*   If OpenAPI configuration is disabled in PostgREST, but you are trying to access the API root path (e.g., `/`), it can result in an \"Invalid path\" error with code `PGRST126`[1][2].\n\n**Debugging Steps:**\n*   **Check PostgREST Logs:** PostgREST logs basic request information, including the URL requested and HTTP response status[6]. This can provide valuable insights into what URL PostgREST is actually receiving.\n*   **Verify PostgREST Version:** Knowing your PostgREST version is important for checking documentation and known issues[7][6].\n*   **Database Logs:** Configure PostgreSQL to log statements to understand how HTTP requests are being translated into SQL commands[7].\n*   **Direct cURL Requests:** Use `curl` to make direct requests to PostgREST to rule out issues with your client-side code or libraries.\n*   **Examine the PostgREST Error Code:** PostgREST errors often follow a PostgreSQL-like structure, including `MESSAGE`, `DETAIL`, `HINT`, and `ERRCODE`[1]. The specific error code `PGRST125` directly corresponds to \"Invalid path is specified in request URL\"[2].\n\nBy carefully reviewing the URL construction, database schema, PostgREST configuration, and any reverse proxy settings, you can typically identify and resolve the \"Invalid path specified in request URL\" error.\n\nSources:\n[1] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHMKQJpzlXzACcgEV202VBsScuwKKZP8jrHkgzd2C-UEYbJkZX_9BvzRXJb02G2AFuZfG0r5C4zZDqY0PqW71byuut0gfONyWFp3eHaFirvrAcZ8Yl0qqMQvA-vYEsjcKKVWjWoHdM8hicNdKfXU-YUsgGhu_k=)\n[2] [supabase.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE45uVrzO8uVQM9ixBs4dKtHUXgKhnAXN0XfgV-cwcns1mK065tcR8-VMV1GH2U05CFWgoT1yaPRcReFI0wAyekroJzjVBBziAYfIGKWSwqBNoojzWKmMZ-cwO41pV5QPm4QWRGPfYEmTWpTCnVjiKMuAEiCiPeEs7Z)\n[3] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEysf7nqpa-udE7-3aoGJhwnNKQNGYrwihp6CAVlWDT-8v7-4X0YK7r2ihWB2hLZgezsHQvK675hJ9A7dMtYHDOK4cC743IIcobcJ8QIXZTapQ5MlpxIgKhRCf_EYCrdE_sXwvycRDvnazgDqPQ946gD0zXYf3-aerwlPo=)\n[4] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEbJfkzvEJUjiabJe5YB92Cbz20onaBla1-qVlg4Tl5UkKRY0SVjYM0D9jzw41pJ0Jc2iNssAamISAHF2Bs4C9uirayfhQ-MsrnNhRbZBVT10OPjcGF_OnFsgIww5IS5udbgQemws4u5-h3ioIE)\n[5] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHhrc8iYImj1_J7Q2b_oz5SUFJ7MAdRkh_7qPjjEOm8quIwd-ylKvS1_cCEI_WHTjT4fitgb3XN-m62fEz7A4HAahUVoZZH3Zxy0FOYKf2Cvs875pBGAr25YBPKpmiANIgFhhNvergIE88Crq5MRGi0Vw==)\n[6] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFfL7x2FyzYWNMgQpiFP50Xz7Ra8xCYH8lCYYlZ6YDjhqdLP21OeeILl6w4iiPFbx0zUnLtpNxaFfhMOlu5K3zYQTWFKe34Nj8U4_iHtUvvdoyEJmUv-eyzakdvyro9DsfhKzoGdHId7QPFX6P6qFmenSu9up6HVnhU)\n[7] [postgrest.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGV8QtrysnQiOQ9pF7jaKpf9bCEvffIixLiWuKoNiUx5QLUgb5ZCG2-D_7Xl2WKB5eLIJD9mLrLae8BlFWA-3mG_3fNQ8XBY8U_5P60FCIc1XAUiAm-5kQ2GWkiamu47FHBMrVV3qiFUq7i59E=)"
    search_type: SEARCH_WEB_TYPE_WEB
