    summary: "Tailwind CSS v4 introduces a new approach to managing variants like dark mode, shifting towards a \"CSS-first\" configuration where the `tailwind.config.js` file is largely replaced by CSS directives[1][2][3][4].\n\nKey aspects of `@variant dark` in Tailwind CSS v4:\n*   **CSS-first Configuration** In Tailwind CSS v4, the configuration, including variants, is primarily handled within your CSS file using `@theme` and `@custom-variant` (or `@variant`) directives[1][2][4][5][6]. This means you no longer rely on JavaScript configuration for defining your dark mode strategy[2][7].\n*   **Default Dark Mode Behavior** By default, Tailwind v4 uses the user\'s operating system preference via the `prefers-color-scheme` CSS media feature. The `dark:` variant is automatically active when the system is set to dark mode without any additional configuration[7][8].\n*   **Manual Dark Mode Toggling** To implement a manual dark mode toggle (e.g., using a button), you can override the default `dark` variant in your CSS using `@custom-variant` or `@variant dark`. This allows you to define a custom selector, such as `.dark` on the `<html>` element, to activate dark mode styles[9][7][6][10][8][11].\n    *   For example, you can define a custom variant in your CSS like `@custom-variant dark (&:is(.dark *));` or `@variant dark (.dark &);` to apply styles when a parent element has the `.dark` class[9][6]. This ensures that child elements automatically inherit dark mode styles[9].\n    *   You can then use JavaScript to toggle the `.dark` class on the `<html>` element to switch between themes[7][10][8].\n*   **CSS Variables for Theming** Tailwind CSS v4 makes all of your design tokens available as CSS variables by default. This makes it easier to define dynamic themes (light/dark/system) and reuse values at runtime using just CSS[1][12][7][4]. You can define theme tokens within `@theme` and override them for specific modes within `@layer base`[7][13][11].\n*   **Simplified Usage** Once the `@variant dark` is configured, you can use the `dark:` prefix in your HTML classes exactly as you would in previous versions to apply dark mode-specific styles[7][10]. For example, `dark:bg-gray-900` will apply a dark gray background when dark mode is active[10].\n\nThe transition to a CSS-first configuration and the new variant handling in Tailwind CSS v4 aim to improve performance and leverage modern CSS features[1][2][3][4].\n\nSources:\n[1] [tailwindcss.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG45iRqjuhbFqHiqOHp6AmCFTH0OcmsWZo21C7l9czMOCbZ0Z3t2vY25QXVnvyyC5J2o43O33O8o6jt1Da19YMJea2oTmVI7pyZjo9-6W2uupfZqB9fmwmdbN8Yw14i80dbDMJTi9o=)\n[2] [digitalapplied.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGze7JEABkU3M1-dvwBT15jjBKTIDtyheG78vWsKa8tkVnOnwUQcTOD1KaFj95jvXyXu8ikmbt2_MS3I1Mqq9AE9YjHzI83oKAYlG4Am82OeJJUkKtDTkYpdjlOCDb5ymFGqSV158KhZGAlX8iO8UwrsDKAt_EpDYazohx42TtDbXIbRi_QGVcJxwv9)\n[3] [tailkits.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF_hZYv1lTcCu2Hp1Ob2pHGUgzNDppMoGk5m_GuEpPhI3GPaHciLo7mt-4Sad0dEjjpmvvwS1_Jf-SXMd7QDD1fT5PDwz9OAa-PnLnSE0SZLVvJ5vWkKx0_mA_KZlNBJzMoLVg9f8ZV9-i-tXHPpHMEexqCpsS0)\n[4] [medium.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGN3LvXWzPBRk1cUScVuznYpMros8Bj1grWPED1Lf3zEbx4xDr4Dcnx66W8j2AgtqW55I2DTeNqf9ShcXyaqzJ-tpR7FTg0DTZ7lCPeTcU2M8ku4v-duo13mtknZZPPZrqjLGZ1q5mwF7fv6dVWlaRHig97YqudbzY8EsToYy50a9sZu31VabSScD4oYZAaCBEhjhrSTIxRX2aXYi1MTuYcPWbV_svjelzvoA==)\n[5] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH7pfhzuqZ-EuaU5xd4-tM_6evQPtDXzwn2UGx3dX6QxhtpS01s0QRy6NtoG8IwbVwm6fl7-QRLng1XUO-vDwc7E3Sikw-0UFvCY2_iZ_S8jD8juY8DKg-8_jXB2lOjLFr7t0pXGqbRHdyQmCx9jhSBDaKgWtHQEY3Ohby8)\n[6] [medium.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEoaU0fLN46PPja2zvW-LQd9mzC4Ydy-yNuNQopRMhGs8-6gZ4rV7SjuU_UKx0SXWd1I5dvmDdwTEXGy0JvMOqHG-pkjiUSgDTVKiYvAF9MLe02tmCmzuvFfQ2j